meta {
  name: change-password
  type: http
  seq: 5
}

post {
  url: {{vipa}}/api/v1/auth/password
  body: json
  auth: inherit
}

headers {
  X-XSRF-TOKEN: {{csrfToken}}
  Content-Type: application/json
}

body:json {
  {
    "currentPassword": "{{adminPassword}}",
    "newPassword": "{{newAdminPassword}}"
  }
}

settings {
  encodeUrl: true
  timeout: 0
}

docs {
  Changes the password of the account the session belongs to. Run `login` first — this
  answers 401 without a session, and 403 without a CSRF token like any other write.
  
  Answers 204 with no body. The account acted on is the one in the session, and there is
  no field for another: this endpoint cannot change somebody else's password, by
  construction rather than by a check.
  
  Failures:
  
    400  the current password does not match, or the new one breaks a rule (shorter than
         12 characters, or equal to the current one). The message names the rule.
    409  the account has no `local` identity — nothing here owns its password. From
         phase 7 that means a Microsoft account, which changes its password at Microsoft.
  
  Running this one invalidates the user's *other* sessions and keeps the caller's, so
  `me` still answers 200 right after. Checking the invalidation needs a second client
  holding its own JSESSIONID — a private browser window is enough; Bruno has a single
  cookie jar and cannot hold two sessions for the same host at once.
  
  Both passwords come from secret variables in the Localhost environment — Bruno stores
  their values on your machine and commits only the names, so a real password never
  reaches the repository. Set {{adminPassword}} to the account's current password and
  {{newAdminPassword}} to what you want it to become.

  It is not idempotent, which is easy to forget when re-running a collection: once this
  has succeeded, {{adminPassword}} names a password that is no longer current, and the
  second run answers 400. **After a successful run, copy {{newAdminPassword}} into
  {{adminPassword}}** and give the new one a fresh value. Both edits happen in the
  environment editor; nothing in this file changes, which is the point — before this, the
  two values lived here and went stale in a tracked file.

  To test the 400 instead, put a deliberately wrong value in {{adminPassword}} for one
  run. A rejected change writes nothing, so the real password is unaffected and
  {{adminPassword}} can go straight back afterwards.
}
